1
00:00:09,843 --> 00:00:15,769
[SPEAKER_00]: There's the Dhammapada verse, which I think deals with what I want to be talking about now.

2
00:00:16,189 --> 00:00:19,952
[SPEAKER_00]: And, uh, it's number 11 and 12.

3
00:00:21,614 --> 00:00:34,706
[SPEAKER_00]: Those who mistake the unessential to be essential and the essential to be unessential, dwelling in wrong purposeful thoughts, never arrive at the essential.

4
00:00:36,237 --> 00:00:44,740
[SPEAKER_00]: Those who know the essential to be essential, and the unessential to be unessential, dwelling in right purposeful thoughts, arrive at the essential.

5
00:00:45,540 --> 00:00:47,381
[SPEAKER_00]: Sama-saṅkhapa.

6
00:00:47,461 --> 00:00:49,562
[SPEAKER_00]: That's right purposeful thoughts.

7
00:00:52,843 --> 00:01:05,207
[SPEAKER_00]: So, knowing what you want, knowing the right... being clear on your purpose, what you really want, say, freedom from suffering.

8
00:01:07,477 --> 00:01:11,759
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the most fundamental context of the holy life.

9
00:01:12,559 --> 00:01:14,480
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the freedom from suffering.

10
00:01:14,700 --> 00:01:22,003
[SPEAKER_01]: As the Buddha said himself, like the sea has only one taste of salt, so the dhamma has only one taste of freedom from suffering.

11
00:01:24,984 --> 00:01:28,906
[SPEAKER_01]: And again, you can take that in a sort of general descriptive manner.

12
00:01:29,026 --> 00:01:30,247
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, freedom from suffering.

13
00:01:30,667 --> 00:01:33,068
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can also take that in a manner of quite a

14
00:01:33,804 --> 00:01:36,885
[SPEAKER_01]: It's quite an acute form of correct contemplation.

15
00:01:37,605 --> 00:01:41,847
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, develop the theme of being free from suffering.

16
00:01:41,907 --> 00:01:42,687
[SPEAKER_01]: Contemplate it.

17
00:01:43,848 --> 00:01:48,889
[SPEAKER_01]: Maintain it, sort of, maintain it in your mind.

18
00:01:49,149 --> 00:01:51,670
[SPEAKER_01]: Have it endure in your mind.

19
00:01:51,710 --> 00:01:52,731
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't forget about it.

20
00:01:52,891 --> 00:01:54,171
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't lose perspective on it.

21
00:01:55,992 --> 00:01:59,073
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you had faith to begin with,

22
00:02:00,463 --> 00:02:04,024
[SPEAKER_01]: In the Buddhist teaching, they offer you possible way out of suffering.

23
00:02:04,625 --> 00:02:10,847
[SPEAKER_01]: That means you will also, if you don't lose perspective, have faith in all the other instructions He gave.

24
00:02:11,727 --> 00:02:14,408
[SPEAKER_01]: The reasons for celibacy, the reasons for virtue.

25
00:02:15,689 --> 00:02:20,830
[SPEAKER_01]: It won't be as hard, because you have faith, and you will see it pertaining to the goal, because you have that perspective.

26
00:02:20,850 --> 00:02:27,633
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why faith is also... could also be enough for somebody to make effort.

27
00:02:28,098 --> 00:02:37,382
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't necessarily need to have it all figured out, but if you have faith in the right instruction, and you do it rightly, but that doesn't mean you can sacrifice your perspective on it.

28
00:02:38,842 --> 00:02:44,644
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the faith can help you if the perspective hasn't been compromised, if the purpose hasn't been obscured.

29
00:02:45,805 --> 00:02:46,665
[SPEAKER_01]: Why are you doing this?

30
00:02:46,765 --> 00:02:47,505
[SPEAKER_01]: Why are you a monk?

31
00:02:47,565 --> 00:02:49,046
[SPEAKER_01]: Or why are you practicing dhamma?

32
00:02:49,166 --> 00:02:50,266
[SPEAKER_01]: To be free from suffering.

33
00:02:50,607 --> 00:02:52,687
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you always keep that at the back of your mind.

34
00:02:53,588 --> 00:02:54,248
[SPEAKER_01]: That purpose.

35
00:02:57,467 --> 00:03:05,412
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you can get to differentiate more and more things that are then compatible with it and things that are not incompatible.

36
00:03:05,772 --> 00:03:09,914
[SPEAKER_01]: And above all, that's how you can also become aware of your own responsibility for it.

37
00:03:12,396 --> 00:03:15,938
[SPEAKER_01]: Like your own habits of craving that revolve around random things.

38
00:03:15,958 --> 00:03:17,398
[SPEAKER_01]: You'll say, wait a minute, why am I doing this?

39
00:03:18,319 --> 00:03:19,340
[SPEAKER_01]: Because now you have perspective.

40
00:03:19,360 --> 00:03:22,041
[SPEAKER_01]: This is what's important to me, to be free from suffering.

41
00:03:22,741 --> 00:03:23,542
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, here I am,

42
00:03:24,214 --> 00:03:27,657
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, feverishly engaged with this, trying to... What's the purpose of that?

43
00:03:28,297 --> 00:03:30,099
[SPEAKER_01]: And I can't seem to be able to stop.

44
00:03:30,339 --> 00:03:31,020
[SPEAKER_01]: Why not?

45
00:03:31,060 --> 00:03:32,080
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you lost perspective.

46
00:03:32,100 --> 00:03:37,084
[SPEAKER_01]: You regain perspective, stopping, restraining, becomes effortless, or certainly requires less effort.

47
00:03:38,486 --> 00:03:41,848
[SPEAKER_00]: So, gaining perspective makes things a lot easier.

48
00:03:42,989 --> 00:03:43,790
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, yeah, yeah.

49
00:03:43,810 --> 00:03:48,093
[SPEAKER_01]: Maintain... gaining perspective and maintaining perspective makes things more easier.

50
00:03:48,133 --> 00:03:48,694
[SPEAKER_01]: Because why?

51
00:03:48,774 --> 00:03:53,878
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the purpose is not obscured, or it's regenerated.

52
00:03:54,509 --> 00:04:13,264
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, like even, as we mentioned that the other day, like even in the lay world of people not necessarily interested in practicing Buddhism, like the motivational speakers and so on, talk about finding the purpose for your life, because without it, you're going to be depressed, or you'll be just wandering aimlessly, or just scratching your central itch, day in, day out, nothing.

53
00:04:13,664 --> 00:04:15,986
[SPEAKER_01]: But finding a purpose gives you that meaning.

54
00:04:16,459 --> 00:04:25,024
[SPEAKER_01]: Which then makes actions much more easier to do, duty easier to do, restraint, enduring things much easier to do, because you have a greater purpose.

55
00:04:28,206 --> 00:04:36,731
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can make a practice out of maintaining, through contemplation, maintaining in the back of your mind, not actively, I want to be free from suffering, I want to be free from suffering.

56
00:04:37,131 --> 00:04:41,173
[SPEAKER_01]: Just remind yourself and then let it endure without forgetting about it.

57
00:04:41,574 --> 00:04:44,075
[SPEAKER_01]: Not actively thinking about it, but not forgetting about it either.

58
00:04:44,387 --> 00:04:47,008
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the practice of mindfulness in a nutshell.

59
00:04:47,048 --> 00:04:51,748
[SPEAKER_01]: You could ask yourself, from time to time, maybe, what is the point?

60
00:04:55,549 --> 00:04:58,370
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the point of practice?

61
00:04:59,290 --> 00:04:59,850
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, sure.

62
00:05:00,090 --> 00:05:05,151
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you can, like, re-evaluate, not in a sense of re-evaluate, oh, what am I, what am I doing?

63
00:05:05,171 --> 00:05:07,331
[SPEAKER_01]: But even that, if necessary, sometimes.

64
00:05:08,552 --> 00:05:10,072
[SPEAKER_01]: But you want to be really just...

65
00:05:11,643 --> 00:05:17,645
[SPEAKER_01]: Maintaining the direction of your practice, that you already know, that's the whole point.

66
00:05:18,706 --> 00:05:24,628
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's... that's... that practice of questioning we discussed before.

67
00:05:27,849 --> 00:05:34,712
[SPEAKER_01]: You question yourself as a form of contemplation, not to necessarily find the right answer, but more like to induce the right attitude.

68
00:05:35,152 --> 00:05:40,674
[SPEAKER_01]: They will then bring that perspective back, or clarify it, or introduce new perspectives.

69
00:05:42,158 --> 00:05:46,240
[SPEAKER_01]: You won't be given the right answer, because it's not like a quiz question.

70
00:05:46,881 --> 00:05:51,203
[SPEAKER_01]: It's more like discerning the things that are already there, but you need to see them with the right type of eyes.

71
00:05:52,284 --> 00:05:53,425
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to recognize them.

72
00:05:55,806 --> 00:06:00,389
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to recognize the nature of impermanence, the nature of non-ownership and so on.

73
00:06:00,889 --> 00:06:02,090
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all there already.

74
00:06:02,950 --> 00:06:07,353
[SPEAKER_01]: But not recognizing it means it's not there for you, because it's not being seen.

75
00:06:09,494 --> 00:06:10,675
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you have a question like

76
00:06:11,746 --> 00:06:16,209
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't even need to be anything specific or technical.

77
00:06:16,229 --> 00:06:20,391
[SPEAKER_01]: Just as we said, you remind yourself or you ask yourself, why am I practicing?

78
00:06:21,692 --> 00:06:22,392
[SPEAKER_01]: What's the purpose?

79
00:06:23,272 --> 00:06:26,354
[SPEAKER_01]: I've been doing this Buddhist practice for a number of years.

80
00:06:26,434 --> 00:06:28,055
[SPEAKER_01]: What's the purpose of that?

81
00:06:28,075 --> 00:06:31,157
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter how far you got into that practice, how much you know.

82
00:06:31,477 --> 00:06:34,178
[SPEAKER_01]: You can always re-ask yourself that question.

83
00:06:34,779 --> 00:06:40,422
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you do it rightly, you will just re-establish the perspective or just remind yourself that that perspective is still there.

84
00:06:42,144 --> 00:06:43,885
[SPEAKER_01]: Either way, it's going to result in mindfulness.

85
00:06:45,486 --> 00:06:49,568
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if your question is, why am I practicing?

86
00:06:50,629 --> 00:06:54,471
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether you're a beginner or not a beginner, you ask yourself that, and don't jump at the answer.

87
00:06:54,571 --> 00:06:54,971
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't rush.

88
00:06:55,211 --> 00:06:56,292
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, because of this... No.

89
00:06:56,612 --> 00:06:58,553
[SPEAKER_01]: Just, what's the point of this?

90
00:06:59,954 --> 00:07:01,514
[SPEAKER_01]: And you'll see that the answer is already there.

91
00:07:02,295 --> 00:07:04,076
[SPEAKER_01]: Otherwise, you wouldn't have been doing it for all these years.

92
00:07:04,716 --> 00:07:09,138
[SPEAKER_01]: The point is, as the point always was, freedom from suffering.

93
00:07:09,158 --> 00:07:11,780
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you don't feel, perhaps, that you're free from suffering enough,

94
00:07:12,493 --> 00:07:13,954
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you can press the questions further.

95
00:07:14,234 --> 00:07:27,441
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, not just so that you can find the right answer that will please you, but more like that, through that attitude of questioning, you will start to look in the direction, eh... different from the ready-made answer that would come your way.

96
00:07:29,102 --> 00:07:33,784
[SPEAKER_01]: The question that will inevitably then have to result in in greater perspective, eventually.

97
00:07:33,844 --> 00:07:40,968
[SPEAKER_01]: It might be more ambiguous perspective, and that's what people fear, to question their own beliefs, their own efforts, because

98
00:07:41,797 --> 00:07:50,441
[SPEAKER_01]: By putting it in question, means you're already questioning... well, you're already implying that it could have also been invalid.

99
00:07:51,522 --> 00:07:52,742
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I must not question it then.

100
00:07:53,262 --> 00:07:56,544
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how I will not think about a possibility of me being wrong, for example.

101
00:08:00,045 --> 00:08:03,147
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you always question yourself, that becomes your perspective.

102
00:08:03,247 --> 00:08:07,609
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how actually you cannot be affected, if a thing does change.

103
00:08:07,972 --> 00:08:15,254
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you haven't been emotionally invested in it and certainly you haven't invested like a form of... well, invested by ignorance.

104
00:08:15,274 --> 00:08:16,155
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, oh, I ignore this.

105
00:08:16,195 --> 00:08:17,215
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not going to question this.

106
00:08:17,835 --> 00:08:21,837
[SPEAKER_01]: Because simply putting it in question, that ambiguity causes me anxiety.

107
00:08:23,157 --> 00:08:24,377
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why I will not question it.

108
00:08:25,798 --> 00:08:33,561
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's completely the opposite attitude of the attitude you should have if you want to be free from suffering.

109
00:08:33,661 --> 00:08:36,482
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you give me an example?

110
00:08:36,822 --> 00:08:37,802
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the example would be...

111
00:08:38,427 --> 00:08:39,347
[SPEAKER_01]: Why am I practicing?

112
00:08:41,168 --> 00:08:43,589
[SPEAKER_01]: Because I want to be free from suffering.

113
00:08:43,609 --> 00:08:46,050
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I free from suffering?

114
00:08:46,131 --> 00:08:50,412
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if the answer to that is no... You know the answer.

115
00:08:50,473 --> 00:08:51,253
[SPEAKER_01]: You know the answer.

116
00:08:51,333 --> 00:08:53,434
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I fundamentally, ultimately beyond suffering?

117
00:08:54,274 --> 00:08:55,495
[SPEAKER_01]: If the answer is yes, great.

118
00:08:55,555 --> 00:08:56,835
[SPEAKER_01]: You already know what you're doing.

119
00:08:57,356 --> 00:09:04,919
[SPEAKER_01]: But the answer is no, or I'm not sure, means, okay, so all these efforts I've been making, yet I'm still not free from suffering.

120
00:09:05,940 --> 00:09:06,940
[SPEAKER_01]: Perhaps I was wrong.

121
00:09:08,427 --> 00:09:13,411
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like that the Buddha's teaching was wrong, but perhaps I haven't been doing it rightly.

122
00:09:13,431 --> 00:09:28,181
[SPEAKER_01]: And again, you might now, because, you know, all the investment of your effort and emotionally charged practice that you've been doing for a number of years, it will make that person uncomfortable, if you start questioning it like that.

123
00:09:28,481 --> 00:09:29,622
[SPEAKER_01]: It's going to make you uncomfortable.

124
00:09:30,122 --> 00:09:36,187
[SPEAKER_01]: What if these 20 years of my meditation methods and techniques, and I'm not free from suffering?

125
00:09:36,467 --> 00:09:37,788
[SPEAKER_01]: What if I've been doing it wrong?

126
00:09:38,830 --> 00:09:39,750
[SPEAKER_00]: Could I start again?

127
00:09:40,571 --> 00:09:47,014
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the sooner you start that questioning, the less of an emotional investment it will become, if you've been doing it wrongly.

128
00:09:47,955 --> 00:10:00,261
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, if you question it from the start, or if you start questioning it as soon as you become aware of that you should question it, it's still a good thing, because it will prevent you from proliferating further, if it were the wrong way.

129
00:10:00,481 --> 00:10:05,484
[SPEAKER_01]: And again, if it were not the wrong way, you would have been free from suffering by now.

130
00:10:10,220 --> 00:10:14,762
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then you re-evaluate, but not like, oh, but what if... yeah, I might have been wrong.

131
00:10:14,982 --> 00:10:16,563
[SPEAKER_01]: But again, what is my purpose?

132
00:10:17,103 --> 00:10:21,945
[SPEAKER_01]: To never be wrong, or to be free from suffering?

133
00:10:21,985 --> 00:10:22,825
[SPEAKER_01]: To be free from suffering.

134
00:10:22,845 --> 00:10:25,066
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it doesn't matter even if for a hundred years I've been wrong.

135
00:10:26,427 --> 00:10:27,548
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, I can be right again.

136
00:10:28,048 --> 00:10:29,889
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, I know that everything I've done is wrong.

137
00:10:31,229 --> 00:10:35,471
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, it's less likely that I'll repeat the same mistakes, because I admitted it to myself.

138
00:10:35,941 --> 00:10:45,548
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you never question it, never admit it, you will then blindly just go and keep repeating the same thing, that you deep down inside know it doesn't work, because it hasn't free you from suffering after all this time.

139
00:10:46,589 --> 00:10:51,213
[SPEAKER_01]: And even Ajahn Chah said himself, if you don't become a Sotāpanna in the first five years of practice, you're not doing it right.

140
00:10:53,034 --> 00:10:58,338
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't get the right view within the first five years of becoming a monk, committed, you're not doing it right.

141
00:10:59,439 --> 00:11:00,219
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's quite accurate.

142
00:11:00,700 --> 00:11:01,520
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not doing it right.

143
00:11:01,600 --> 00:11:03,622
[SPEAKER_01]: And you should really, like, re-evaluate quickly,

144
00:11:04,254 --> 00:11:07,557
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the more you invest in the wrong effort, the harder it will be to undo it.

145
00:11:07,657 --> 00:11:13,202
[SPEAKER_01]: Because more conceit gets involved, more sense of self, and also, like, more of that, like, oh, I'm not gonna look at this.

146
00:11:14,543 --> 00:11:20,868
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's becoming... the more you ignore it, the more unpleasant it will be to face it later on.

147
00:11:20,908 --> 00:11:21,669
[SPEAKER_01]: So, a simple question.

148
00:11:21,909 --> 00:11:22,910
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I free from suffering?

149
00:11:24,331 --> 00:11:28,775
[SPEAKER_01]: If you know you're not free from suffering, you can, as you said, you can ask yourself, why am I suffering?

150
00:11:29,682 --> 00:11:33,545
[SPEAKER_01]: But again, don't... well, I'm suffering because... and then give some stock phrase answer.

151
00:11:33,885 --> 00:11:34,005
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

152
00:11:34,205 --> 00:11:37,127
[SPEAKER_01]: Ask yourself, why am I suffering?

153
00:11:38,588 --> 00:11:41,370
[SPEAKER_01]: Without... and then, like, restrain the answer.

154
00:11:41,610 --> 00:11:42,231
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't answer it.

155
00:11:42,331 --> 00:11:43,612
[SPEAKER_01]: Just let the question endure.

156
00:11:46,133 --> 00:11:48,175
[SPEAKER_01]: Just remind yourself, why am I suffering?

157
00:11:49,155 --> 00:11:49,956
[SPEAKER_01]: And let it endure.

158
00:11:51,057 --> 00:11:57,381
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it will prevent... by allowing the question to endure, not jumping into an answer, it will... it forces you.

159
00:11:58,372 --> 00:12:02,455
[SPEAKER_01]: To adopt a perspective by refraining from jumping into the answer.

160
00:12:03,736 --> 00:12:07,959
[SPEAKER_01]: And that perspective, it's going to be ambiguous, it's going to be unpleasant, but it's still a perspective.

161
00:12:09,040 --> 00:12:13,583
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's still better than just providing a quick answer that's going to turn a blind eye to any greater perspective.

162
00:12:14,564 --> 00:12:14,744
[SPEAKER_00]: So...

163
00:12:14,864 --> 00:12:18,207
[SPEAKER_00]: It's similar to like a non-judgmental...

164
00:12:18,347 --> 00:12:20,869
[SPEAKER_01]: It is, but not judgmental for the sake of non-judgment.

165
00:12:20,969 --> 00:12:21,369
[SPEAKER_00]: Non-biased.

166
00:12:21,389 --> 00:12:22,970
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, oh, I'm non-judgmental.

167
00:12:23,191 --> 00:12:26,473
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that can become your judgment, because you started doing it mechanically.

168
00:12:26,834 --> 00:12:28,555
[SPEAKER_01]: If you ask yourself, am I judgmental?

169
00:12:29,536 --> 00:12:30,336
[SPEAKER_01]: And don't answer it.

170
00:12:32,357 --> 00:12:33,678
[SPEAKER_01]: Certainly don't answer it right away.

171
00:12:34,639 --> 00:12:36,980
[SPEAKER_01]: That's going to force you to endure with that ambiguity.

172
00:12:37,200 --> 00:12:38,641
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I yes or am I not?

173
00:12:40,102 --> 00:12:45,426
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, see, saying, am I judgmental?

174
00:12:45,946 --> 00:12:47,567
[SPEAKER_01]: Saying yes, means you're judgmental.

175
00:12:47,667 --> 00:12:49,388
[SPEAKER_01]: Saying no, means you're judgmental.

176
00:12:50,629 --> 00:12:51,229
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I judgmental?

177
00:12:51,309 --> 00:12:53,251
[SPEAKER_01]: No, I'm not judgmental.

178
00:12:53,311 --> 00:12:55,492
[SPEAKER_01]: You just made the judgment that you are not judgmental.

179
00:12:56,304 --> 00:12:56,704
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I mean.

180
00:12:56,724 --> 00:12:57,564
[SPEAKER_01]: Why am I suffering?

181
00:12:57,624 --> 00:13:01,906
[SPEAKER_01]: But don't answer it in that sense, but simply look at the question of why am I suffering?

182
00:13:01,926 --> 00:13:05,107
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't try to collect information about it, but why am I suffering?

183
00:13:05,167 --> 00:13:07,788
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when you ask yourself, why am I suffering?

184
00:13:07,828 --> 00:13:09,389
[SPEAKER_01]: What are you directing the question at?

185
00:13:10,469 --> 00:13:12,490
[SPEAKER_01]: Will you directing it at the actual suffering?

186
00:13:12,590 --> 00:13:13,950
[SPEAKER_01]: Or at the possibility to suffer?

187
00:13:14,110 --> 00:13:15,831
[SPEAKER_01]: Or at the fact that you're not free from suffering?

188
00:13:16,471 --> 00:13:16,751
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

189
00:13:16,771 --> 00:13:18,032
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'm not free from suffering.

190
00:13:18,392 --> 00:13:20,653
[SPEAKER_01]: Otherwise, I wouldn't even ask myself why am I suffering?

191
00:13:21,573 --> 00:13:23,914
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's already a first recognition that comes simply from

192
00:13:24,468 --> 00:13:27,149
[SPEAKER_01]: That right perspective that comes out of the right question.

193
00:13:28,430 --> 00:13:29,650
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why am I suffering then?

194
00:13:31,351 --> 00:13:31,951
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I don't know.

195
00:13:34,392 --> 00:13:35,913
[SPEAKER_01]: Certainly, I cannot define it.

196
00:13:36,253 --> 00:13:37,033
[SPEAKER_01]: Not at this stage.

197
00:13:37,093 --> 00:13:38,594
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why am I suffering then?

198
00:13:39,494 --> 00:13:41,315
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I suffer because suffering is present.

199
00:13:42,755 --> 00:13:44,576
[SPEAKER_01]: Would I suffer if suffering doesn't manifest?

200
00:13:44,976 --> 00:13:45,116
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

201
00:13:46,077 --> 00:13:48,598
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I suffer when suffering is manifest.

202
00:13:48,678 --> 00:13:48,858
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

203
00:13:49,618 --> 00:13:51,039
[SPEAKER_01]: Do I have a say in that, for example?

204
00:13:51,319 --> 00:13:51,879
[SPEAKER_01]: You can ask that.

205
00:13:51,919 --> 00:13:53,840
[SPEAKER_01]: Do I have a say in suffering being manifest?

206
00:13:55,409 --> 00:14:03,375
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I might have say in things I do, but do I have say in the unpleasant feeling being manifested, in enduring?

207
00:14:04,095 --> 00:14:07,898
[SPEAKER_01]: No, because if I had a say, it would disappear whenever I tell it to.

208
00:14:08,078 --> 00:14:10,140
[SPEAKER_01]: It wouldn't even appear, because I don't want it to appear.

209
00:14:10,880 --> 00:14:11,441
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I don't have...

210
00:14:11,501 --> 00:14:13,082
[SPEAKER_01]: I am liable to these feelings.

211
00:14:13,983 --> 00:14:19,747
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, the weather changes, and I can't even conceive stopping or preventing it.

212
00:14:21,008 --> 00:14:21,188
[SPEAKER_01]: So,

213
00:14:21,778 --> 00:14:26,621
[SPEAKER_01]: Is then that disagreeable feeling, is that my suffering?

214
00:14:26,661 --> 00:14:37,666
[SPEAKER_01]: Is the disagreeable mood that comes my way, that I have no say in it, and I can't do anything about it, is that why be... is that because of... why... like... is that the reason why I suffer?

215
00:14:43,169 --> 00:14:45,070
[SPEAKER_01]: And again, yes, no, don't jump there.

216
00:14:45,230 --> 00:14:48,832
[SPEAKER_01]: Is the... is... are my disagreeable feelings, is that what my suffering is?

217
00:14:50,013 --> 00:14:50,633
[SPEAKER_01]: Let that endure.

218
00:14:50,936 --> 00:14:53,817
[SPEAKER_01]: That didn't endure for days, if nothing presents itself.

219
00:14:55,538 --> 00:15:06,462
[SPEAKER_01]: By having perspective on such questioning, that pertains to these universal natures, so to speak, you are looking in that direction, which means, there is a chance of you discerning things rightly.

220
00:15:07,363 --> 00:15:12,745
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you just give a stock answer, or the answer you think it should be, you stop looking, because you provided the answer.

221
00:15:15,086 --> 00:15:18,947
[SPEAKER_01]: And even, oh yeah, this answer is perfectly right, fine, but let me keep looking.

222
00:15:21,783 --> 00:15:38,334
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why we're meant by self-interrogation and questioning, or then you go and visit people who think they're wiser than you, or you know they're wiser, and let them interrogate and question, because it will bring all these possibilities of developing these perspectives through self-questioning, that undoes the ignorance, undoes the lust and aversion.

223
00:15:41,656 --> 00:15:42,736
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why am I suffering?

224
00:15:43,697 --> 00:15:45,198
[SPEAKER_01]: Why am I not free from suffering?

225
00:15:52,898 --> 00:15:54,399
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't have to answer it.

226
00:15:55,419 --> 00:15:56,399
[SPEAKER_01]: Just become fully aware of it.

227
00:15:56,799 --> 00:15:59,180
[SPEAKER_01]: Not for some sort of mystical revelation.

228
00:15:59,880 --> 00:16:03,702
[SPEAKER_01]: Simply, you want that perspective or that questioning, existential questioning, to endure.

229
00:16:05,382 --> 00:16:07,423
[SPEAKER_01]: Because by enduring, you become more familiar with it.

230
00:16:09,043 --> 00:16:10,364
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why am I not free from suffering?

231
00:16:10,784 --> 00:16:13,825
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, let's get some guidelines in regard to that.

232
00:16:13,845 --> 00:16:14,625
[SPEAKER_01]: What did the Buddha say?

233
00:16:15,646 --> 00:16:17,326
[SPEAKER_01]: Why are you not free from suffering?

234
00:16:17,426 --> 00:16:18,547
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there is craving.

235
00:16:18,627 --> 00:16:19,087
[SPEAKER_01]: You crave.

236
00:16:19,627 --> 00:16:22,768
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, so I'm not free from suffering because I still crave.

237
00:16:23,577 --> 00:16:24,618
[SPEAKER_01]: Craving in regard to what?

238
00:16:25,178 --> 00:16:30,563
[SPEAKER_01]: But craving is on the level of your doing, not on the level of some placement.

239
00:16:31,103 --> 00:16:33,445
[SPEAKER_01]: You can say, where is the feeling, in a way?

240
00:16:34,486 --> 00:16:35,767
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you haven't created it.

241
00:16:36,227 --> 00:16:37,148
[SPEAKER_01]: You're liable to it.

242
00:16:37,448 --> 00:16:38,469
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is the craving?

243
00:16:39,230 --> 00:16:40,051
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the craving?

244
00:16:40,091 --> 00:16:40,271
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

245
00:16:40,771 --> 00:16:40,991
[SPEAKER_01]: Any.

246
00:16:41,252 --> 00:16:43,734
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't have to overthink, how shall I ask this question?

247
00:16:43,754 --> 00:16:50,179
[SPEAKER_01]: The point is, questioning the subject, questioning the phenomena, will at least start inducing some perspective on it.

248
00:16:53,473 --> 00:16:55,014
[SPEAKER_01]: So, where is, where is the craving?

249
00:16:55,134 --> 00:16:56,075
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, where is the craving?

250
00:16:56,095 --> 00:16:58,217
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, craving is in regard to the feeling.

251
00:16:59,458 --> 00:17:00,158
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what Buddha said.

252
00:17:00,258 --> 00:17:02,260
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, but now I need to understand that.

253
00:17:02,400 --> 00:17:04,602
[SPEAKER_01]: I can't just give the answer that I read in the suttas.

254
00:17:05,663 --> 00:17:06,944
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is the feeling then?

255
00:17:08,445 --> 00:17:11,667
[SPEAKER_01]: As we just discussed, feeling is that, that I'm liable to.

256
00:17:12,768 --> 00:17:14,510
[SPEAKER_01]: Feeling is that, that I have no say in.

257
00:17:17,512 --> 00:17:22,536
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there are these phenomena, one at a time, pleasant, unpleasant or neutral.

258
00:17:23,280 --> 00:17:25,240
[SPEAKER_01]: They come my way and I have no say in that.

259
00:17:25,320 --> 00:17:26,561
[SPEAKER_01]: I have absolutely no say in that.

260
00:17:26,661 --> 00:17:28,061
[SPEAKER_01]: I cannot prevent them from arising.

261
00:17:28,701 --> 00:17:31,702
[SPEAKER_01]: I cannot tell them to stay longer or to disappear quicker.

262
00:17:32,442 --> 00:17:33,562
[SPEAKER_01]: I am fully liable to it.

263
00:17:34,783 --> 00:17:37,303
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I cannot influence that.

264
00:17:38,264 --> 00:17:44,725
[SPEAKER_01]: But what is it that I can influence on the basis of those those phenomena or feelings?

265
00:17:47,706 --> 00:17:49,226
[SPEAKER_01]: It's your own attitude towards feelings.

266
00:17:49,586 --> 00:17:50,266
[SPEAKER_01]: Your own feelings.

267
00:17:50,367 --> 00:17:52,287
[SPEAKER_01]: Not towards the world that caused these feelings.

268
00:17:53,102 --> 00:17:55,203
[SPEAKER_01]: It's over the feelings that you feel right there and then.

269
00:17:55,884 --> 00:17:59,166
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever present feeling is, it's your attitude in regard to it.

270
00:17:59,906 --> 00:18:00,987
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where craving is.

271
00:18:02,908 --> 00:18:11,113
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you stop resisting the feeling, if you stop indulging the feeling, if you stop ignoring the feeling, you will not be able to crave for the feeling.

272
00:18:12,113 --> 00:18:15,975
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're not able to crave for the feeling, how can you suffer on account of feeling?

273
00:18:16,076 --> 00:18:17,857
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, craving leads to suffering.

274
00:18:18,217 --> 00:18:20,438
[SPEAKER_01]: Absence of craving leads to freedom from suffering.

275
00:18:20,478 --> 00:18:21,599
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm giving answers now.

276
00:18:21,759 --> 00:18:21,959
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure.

277
00:18:22,282 --> 00:18:25,724
[SPEAKER_01]: That came out of questioning the right things.

278
00:18:26,205 --> 00:18:26,565
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course.

279
00:18:26,645 --> 00:18:27,185
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

280
00:18:27,205 --> 00:18:30,067
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you can't just start questioning things rightly without any knowledge.

281
00:18:30,447 --> 00:18:32,709
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to read the suttas, so you know what to question.

282
00:18:32,729 --> 00:18:33,369
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the thing.

283
00:18:33,969 --> 00:18:42,615
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you ask yourself a question about craving, which gives you... makes you look at that.

284
00:18:42,635 --> 00:18:43,075
[SPEAKER_00]: What is that?

285
00:18:43,215 --> 00:18:43,395
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

286
00:18:44,016 --> 00:18:44,496
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

287
00:18:44,516 --> 00:18:47,238
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you see craving.

288
00:18:47,318 --> 00:18:48,959
[SPEAKER_01]: Not in the same sense as you can see, like,

289
00:18:49,400 --> 00:18:50,820
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I'm feeling pleasant, I'm feeling unpleasant.

290
00:18:50,841 --> 00:18:52,321
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah, there is craving right in front of me.

291
00:18:52,521 --> 00:18:54,382
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that would make it an independent phenomenon.

292
00:18:54,662 --> 00:18:56,462
[SPEAKER_01]: In phenomena, it's independent of your attitude.

293
00:18:57,063 --> 00:18:57,563
[SPEAKER_01]: But it isn't.

294
00:18:58,143 --> 00:19:01,684
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why craving or assumptions are negative phenomena, in a sense.

295
00:19:01,904 --> 00:19:06,306
[SPEAKER_01]: You can see that in regard to which your attitude is being sort of exercised.

296
00:19:06,786 --> 00:19:08,406
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you realize my attitude is wrong.

297
00:19:08,446 --> 00:19:10,327
[SPEAKER_01]: So, undoing the attitude undoes the craving.

298
00:19:10,907 --> 00:19:16,149
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Buddha said the upādāna, like the assumption, it's not the five aggregates.

299
00:19:17,274 --> 00:19:20,136
[SPEAKER_01]: But there is no assumption without the five aggregates.

300
00:19:20,176 --> 00:19:21,437
[SPEAKER_01]: You can say the same for craving.

301
00:19:22,438 --> 00:19:26,001
[SPEAKER_01]: Craving is not in regard to the feeling, but there is no craving apart from feeling.

302
00:19:26,562 --> 00:19:31,426
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the desire and lust in regard to feeling that craving is there.

303
00:19:31,446 --> 00:19:35,189
[SPEAKER_01]: It's desire and lust in regard to the five aggregates that upādāna is there.

304
00:19:36,049 --> 00:19:41,734
[SPEAKER_01]: You remove desire and lust, there is no basis for upādāna to be manifested, or there is no basis for craving to be manifested.

305
00:19:42,535 --> 00:19:43,115
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I mean.

306
00:19:43,796 --> 00:19:46,318
[SPEAKER_01]: If you give the positive answer to what craving is,

307
00:19:47,433 --> 00:19:51,174
[SPEAKER_01]: You think that's what craving is, which means you're not looking now on the level of your attitude.

308
00:19:52,274 --> 00:19:53,575
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's a... Am I suffering?

309
00:19:53,755 --> 00:19:53,935
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

310
00:19:54,075 --> 00:19:55,055
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I free from suffering?

311
00:19:55,355 --> 00:19:55,495
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

312
00:19:56,316 --> 00:19:57,196
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the cause of suffering?

313
00:19:57,216 --> 00:19:57,496
[SPEAKER_01]: Craving.

314
00:19:57,556 --> 00:19:57,816
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

315
00:19:58,436 --> 00:20:02,438
[SPEAKER_01]: Now I gave that answer, but now I need to understand that answer.

316
00:20:02,458 --> 00:20:03,918
[SPEAKER_01]: So you can ask yourself, what is my craving?

317
00:20:05,318 --> 00:20:05,879
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I don't know.

318
00:20:06,259 --> 00:20:09,600
[SPEAKER_01]: But I know that whenever suffering is, craving is.

319
00:20:09,840 --> 00:20:10,660
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what the Buddha said.

320
00:20:11,840 --> 00:20:15,622
[SPEAKER_01]: So if I don't suffer, if I'm free from suffering, there is no craving.

321
00:20:15,642 --> 00:20:16,902
[SPEAKER_01]: But the fact I'm not free from suffering,

322
00:20:17,403 --> 00:20:22,347
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whenever there is suffering present in my experience, craving must be there also, to the same extent.

323
00:20:23,208 --> 00:20:25,550
[SPEAKER_01]: So, suffering and craving are inseparable.

324
00:20:25,990 --> 00:20:30,134
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's a conclusion you arrived by not jumping onto the immediate answer.

325
00:20:32,656 --> 00:20:34,918
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I ask, am I suffering?

326
00:20:36,239 --> 00:20:43,205
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, I'm not... it makes me look at that phenomenon which I'm talking about.

327
00:20:43,525 --> 00:20:43,665
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

328
00:20:46,543 --> 00:20:48,584
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why am I suffering?

329
00:20:48,604 --> 00:20:51,285
[SPEAKER_01]: And now you have all this sutras you read about suffering coming up to your mind.

330
00:20:51,305 --> 00:20:51,705
[SPEAKER_01]: That's fine.

331
00:20:52,285 --> 00:20:53,685
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't ignore it, but don't just...

332
00:20:53,785 --> 00:20:55,486
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, I'm suffering because of this.

333
00:20:56,406 --> 00:20:57,727
[SPEAKER_01]: That answer doesn't apply to you.

334
00:20:59,968 --> 00:21:01,328
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'm suffering because of craving.

335
00:21:01,508 --> 00:21:03,349
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, but why am I suffering?

336
00:21:03,929 --> 00:21:06,290
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, so now you can provide even more accurate answer.

337
00:21:06,930 --> 00:21:07,690
[SPEAKER_01]: Why am I suffering?

338
00:21:08,010 --> 00:21:09,531
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, suffering is because of craving.

339
00:21:09,571 --> 00:21:10,911
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why am I suffering?

340
00:21:11,051 --> 00:21:12,852
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, because I haven't understood craving.

341
00:21:14,471 --> 00:21:16,352
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the answer that the Buddha gave, that is correct answer.

342
00:21:16,932 --> 00:21:18,813
[SPEAKER_01]: I haven't understood, which is why I'm suffering.

343
00:21:19,893 --> 00:21:26,096
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that would be more accurate answer to that question, that you arrived at, by not jumping at the first offer answered by your mind.

344
00:21:26,696 --> 00:21:27,336
[SPEAKER_01]: Why am I suffering?

345
00:21:27,376 --> 00:21:28,476
[SPEAKER_01]: Because of craving is present.

346
00:21:28,837 --> 00:21:29,517
[SPEAKER_01]: And saṅkharas.

347
00:21:31,158 --> 00:21:31,378
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

348
00:21:31,898 --> 00:21:34,319
[SPEAKER_01]: Suffering is because of saṅkharas and because of craving.

349
00:21:34,939 --> 00:21:35,899
[SPEAKER_01]: Why am I suffering?

350
00:21:36,300 --> 00:21:38,680
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, because I haven't understood saṅkharas nor the craving.

351
00:21:39,381 --> 00:21:40,641
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why I am suffering.

352
00:21:41,061 --> 00:21:42,562
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why I'm liable to suffering.

353
00:21:44,917 --> 00:21:47,058
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you want to understand it, you start questioning that.

354
00:21:47,198 --> 00:21:47,778
[SPEAKER_01]: What is craving?

355
00:21:47,818 --> 00:21:49,059
[SPEAKER_01]: As you said, where is craving?

356
00:21:49,479 --> 00:21:50,499
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, why do I crave?

357
00:21:50,699 --> 00:21:57,062
[SPEAKER_01]: Any question that will revolve around the phenomenon of craving, at least implies that it designates that direction.

358
00:21:58,583 --> 00:22:04,065
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't try to... oh, well, see, he said this, that answer pleases me, that's why I'm suffering.

359
00:22:04,305 --> 00:22:04,425
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

360
00:22:04,585 --> 00:22:09,187
[SPEAKER_01]: That answer pleases me, maybe for right, maybe for wrong reasons, but that's not why I suffer.

361
00:22:09,567 --> 00:22:12,108
[SPEAKER_01]: I suffer because I haven't understood that answer.

362
00:22:28,890 --> 00:22:34,392
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why it takes effort to develop the skill of contemplating things rightly.

363
00:22:34,412 --> 00:22:36,853
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't just say, oh, why is this happening to me?

364
00:22:37,653 --> 00:22:39,293
[SPEAKER_01]: And expect some wisdom to come out of it.

365
00:22:39,473 --> 00:22:41,194
[SPEAKER_01]: Although, it's a necessary step.

366
00:22:41,914 --> 00:22:45,615
[SPEAKER_01]: If a person doesn't start questioning their own existence, why am I suffering?

367
00:22:46,096 --> 00:22:48,036
[SPEAKER_01]: They'll never look for the way out.

368
00:22:49,277 --> 00:22:51,858
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, yeah, there are people who will never ask that question to themselves.

369
00:22:52,838 --> 00:22:56,259
[SPEAKER_01]: And they'll never ever make any effort towards practice or anything remotely.

370
00:22:57,611 --> 00:22:58,732
[SPEAKER_01]: To free themself from suffering.

371
00:23:02,353 --> 00:23:06,275
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you can also use that as a... as a... what we spoke the other day about the theme.

372
00:23:07,876 --> 00:23:11,778
[SPEAKER_01]: Liability to suffering.

373
00:23:11,798 --> 00:23:14,439
[SPEAKER_01]: You can establish it on like peripheral in your mind.

374
00:23:14,860 --> 00:23:15,680
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't think about it.

375
00:23:15,700 --> 00:23:16,240
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm suffering.

376
00:23:16,280 --> 00:23:21,483
[SPEAKER_01]: I might... Just have it there as a background of whatever you go through in your day-to-day life.

377
00:23:21,583 --> 00:23:22,964
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, suffering might befall me.

378
00:23:24,925 --> 00:23:25,325
[SPEAKER_01]: And again,

379
00:23:27,182 --> 00:23:27,903
[SPEAKER_01]: Anybody can do that.

380
00:23:27,923 --> 00:23:29,544
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need great instruction for it.

381
00:23:29,664 --> 00:23:32,186
[SPEAKER_01]: You just need to be, well, brave in a way.

382
00:23:32,626 --> 00:23:35,368
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to have courage to admit it to yourself that, yes, because it's the truth.

383
00:23:35,989 --> 00:23:42,654
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not that you are fabricating it when you think about it, which is what usually people would develop attitude as a form of self, kind of, defense mechanism.

384
00:23:43,554 --> 00:23:47,517
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, if I think about it, that's how... No, I stop thinking about it and that's how I'm happy.

385
00:23:48,298 --> 00:23:50,579
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how I don't suffer, until you suffer.

386
00:23:51,400 --> 00:23:52,881
[SPEAKER_01]: By then, it's too late to do anything about it.

387
00:23:53,802 --> 00:23:55,203
[SPEAKER_01]: Also, you must not think about suffering

388
00:23:57,773 --> 00:24:01,256
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, whether it's neurotic or not, it's beside the point.

389
00:24:01,276 --> 00:24:02,837
[SPEAKER_01]: The point is that it's the truth.

390
00:24:03,418 --> 00:24:07,301
[SPEAKER_01]: You are liable to suffering at any given moment in your life, in your existence.

391
00:24:08,882 --> 00:24:13,986
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you have that strength to admit that to yourself, gradually, it becomes the theme.

392
00:24:14,767 --> 00:24:21,452
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the theme that the Buddha said it's the necessary theme for somebody to seek the homeless life and practice towards nibbāna.

393
00:24:21,912 --> 00:24:25,335
[SPEAKER_01]: So, a young man realizes he's prey to suffering.

394
00:24:25,795 --> 00:24:26,416
[SPEAKER_01]: He's liable.

395
00:24:27,072 --> 00:24:27,452
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

396
00:24:27,813 --> 00:24:31,915
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, imagine... We spoke about it before, probably, but it doesn't hurt to repeat it.

397
00:24:32,676 --> 00:24:34,957
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, many people would read over that line.

398
00:24:34,977 --> 00:24:36,078
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, he's prey to suffering.

399
00:24:36,098 --> 00:24:36,538
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, yes.

400
00:24:36,638 --> 00:24:38,580
[SPEAKER_01]: But, no, make a theme out of it.

401
00:24:38,600 --> 00:24:39,681
[SPEAKER_01]: Ponder, understand it.

402
00:24:40,181 --> 00:24:42,342
[SPEAKER_01]: How would you feel if you were a prey?

403
00:24:43,383 --> 00:24:52,649
[SPEAKER_01]: If there is a non-specific, non-defined, like, ubiquitous sort of presence of a hunter, but you can't see it, but you know it's hunting you.

404
00:24:55,211 --> 00:24:56,592
[SPEAKER_01]: You would feel like constantly

405
00:24:57,494 --> 00:25:00,815
[SPEAKER_01]: Something can pounce on you, but you can never see from what direction.

406
00:25:02,556 --> 00:25:06,958
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's quite a profound existential sort of anxiety and state.

407
00:25:07,218 --> 00:25:08,899
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Buddha said that is the necessary step.

408
00:25:10,220 --> 00:25:14,321
[SPEAKER_01]: When he recognized, I am prey to suffering, why don't I go and do something about this?

409
00:25:15,422 --> 00:25:16,442
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he didn't fabricate it.

410
00:25:16,782 --> 00:25:19,984
[SPEAKER_01]: He basically discerned that theme, because that theme was the right theme.

411
00:25:20,504 --> 00:25:23,925
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you are truly prey to suffering.

412
00:25:23,965 --> 00:25:24,986
[SPEAKER_01]: And how would you feel if

413
00:25:25,422 --> 00:25:31,624
[SPEAKER_01]: Like you go on throughout the day and some... and people tell you, oh, wait, like there is an assassin stalking you.

414
00:25:32,264 --> 00:25:36,606
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't know when he's gonna hit you, where, nor how, but you know it will.

415
00:25:38,086 --> 00:25:39,567
[SPEAKER_01]: You would be on a constant alert.

416
00:25:41,247 --> 00:25:41,907
[SPEAKER_01]: Involuntarily.

417
00:25:42,147 --> 00:25:45,028
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, it wouldn't be... you don't need to make the effort.

418
00:25:45,048 --> 00:25:47,849
[SPEAKER_01]: You're gonna have to make the effort to distract yourself from it.

419
00:25:49,190 --> 00:25:50,210
[SPEAKER_01]: From the pressing truth.

420
00:25:53,027 --> 00:26:04,129
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why it's hard for people to practice restraint and give up habits and lay life, because that's the... that's part of that self-defense mechanism from admitting the most fundamental basic truth.

421
00:26:04,889 --> 00:26:14,771
[SPEAKER_01]: Then you are not your own self, in the sense of you are not in control of your own existence, which means you are liable to misfortune, to accidents, to... you are liable to... you are prey to suffering.

422
00:26:15,391 --> 00:26:17,751
[SPEAKER_01]: Not to this person or to that... to the government.

423
00:26:17,791 --> 00:26:17,991
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

424
00:26:18,151 --> 00:26:21,732
[SPEAKER_01]: You are prey to the unpleasant feeling arising within yourself.

425
00:26:23,356 --> 00:26:24,837
[SPEAKER_01]: Suffering is hunting you down.

426
00:26:29,538 --> 00:26:32,800
[SPEAKER_01]: And you dwell on that theme, and you're only going to have one result.

427
00:26:34,860 --> 00:26:36,461
[SPEAKER_01]: Seeking out liberation from suffering.

428
00:26:36,481 --> 00:26:36,881
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's...

429
00:26:37,361 --> 00:26:40,503
[SPEAKER_01]: The whole life is about defense mechanism from that.

430
00:26:41,383 --> 00:26:43,524
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why don't you then make the effort... No.

431
00:26:43,844 --> 00:26:48,186
[SPEAKER_01]: What if you were to make the effort and approach it, means no other effort is required.

432
00:26:48,466 --> 00:26:51,007
[SPEAKER_01]: No other maintenance, no other shelter, no other defense mechanism.

433
00:26:51,087 --> 00:26:51,807
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing is required.

434
00:26:52,187 --> 00:26:53,008
[SPEAKER_01]: That's true freedom.

435
00:26:53,048 --> 00:26:54,049
[SPEAKER_01]: No pressure, no burden.

436
00:26:54,109 --> 00:26:55,270
[SPEAKER_01]: I must do this, I must do that.

437
00:26:56,111 --> 00:26:57,772
[SPEAKER_01]: If I don't fix this, suffering will come.

438
00:26:57,832 --> 00:26:59,634
[SPEAKER_01]: If I don't do that, well, that will be unpleasant.

439
00:27:00,314 --> 00:27:03,717
[SPEAKER_01]: No, there's nothing for you to do, because there's nothing for you that can make you suffer.

440
00:27:08,982 --> 00:27:09,683
[SPEAKER_01]: And again, that's not...

441
00:27:10,143 --> 00:27:13,726
[SPEAKER_01]: It can become mindless, conceptualized as theme.

442
00:27:13,746 --> 00:27:14,847
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, I'm suffering, I'm suffering.

443
00:27:15,208 --> 00:27:16,769
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's when you lost your mindfulness.

444
00:27:18,040 --> 00:27:24,502
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you remind yourself that I shouldn't be giving this answer, I should be discerning these answers on the basis of the present situation.

445
00:27:25,402 --> 00:27:31,963
[SPEAKER_01]: Because present situation shows me the nature that I am still liable to suffering.

446
00:27:33,104 --> 00:27:34,224
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means the theme is there.

447
00:27:34,604 --> 00:27:36,804
[SPEAKER_01]: The theme of suffering and freedom from it is already there.

448
00:27:37,665 --> 00:27:39,365
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether you answer it or not is secondary.

449
00:27:50,055 --> 00:27:53,396
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can walk around feeling like you're a prey to suffering.

450
00:27:54,516 --> 00:27:58,578
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter how careful you are, but obviously, it's going to make you more careful.

451
00:27:58,878 --> 00:28:05,100
[SPEAKER_01]: But you realize that no amount of your action can prevent you from experiencing the unpleasant feeling if it chooses to come.

452
00:28:07,200 --> 00:28:15,703
[SPEAKER_01]: So, by admitting this to yourself, then you get to recognize that your defense from unpleasant feeling is not about preventing it to come.

453
00:28:18,172 --> 00:28:22,754
[SPEAKER_01]: Your defense from the unpleasant feeling is about preventing yourself from resisting it.

454
00:28:23,815 --> 00:28:26,436
[SPEAKER_01]: From implying that you can do something about it.

455
00:28:29,917 --> 00:28:31,638
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you prevent yourself from craving.

456
00:28:33,519 --> 00:28:41,223
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you also realize, okay, so if I want to do that, I equally then cannot be careless in regard to pleasant feeling.

457
00:28:41,263 --> 00:28:43,884
[SPEAKER_01]: Because pleasant feeling, unpleasant feeling, it's the feeling.

458
00:28:44,404 --> 00:28:46,385
[SPEAKER_01]: It's that thing I'm trying to free myself from.

459
00:28:48,212 --> 00:28:51,894
[SPEAKER_01]: I can't just free myself from unpleasant feelings and only maintain pleasant feelings.

460
00:28:51,975 --> 00:28:53,936
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll cherry-pick the ones I want.

461
00:28:54,036 --> 00:28:54,176
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

462
00:28:54,776 --> 00:28:56,718
[SPEAKER_01]: Feeling is the problem because I'm liable to it.

463
00:28:57,318 --> 00:29:02,681
[SPEAKER_01]: By accepting the liability to pleasant feeling means I make myself equally liable to the unpleasant feeling.

464
00:29:04,162 --> 00:29:06,444
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where, like, the sensory strain becomes self-evident.

465
00:29:07,625 --> 00:29:12,488
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it reveals feelings.

466
00:29:14,686 --> 00:29:15,206
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

467
00:29:15,406 --> 00:29:19,367
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it reveals the feelings that basically just mirrors, then, what your attitude is there.

468
00:29:19,387 --> 00:29:25,168
[SPEAKER_01]: You realize that, oh, when pleasant feeling is there, my attitude is full acceptance, full welcoming, full delight.

469
00:29:27,069 --> 00:29:31,650
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's the exercising of any attitude towards any feeling.

470
00:29:32,570 --> 00:29:33,891
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why craving is there.

471
00:29:35,331 --> 00:29:36,671
[SPEAKER_01]: So, removing... That's what I mean.

472
00:29:36,851 --> 00:29:44,293
[SPEAKER_01]: You realize... Well, many people, until they realize, will be blaming the feeling, blaming that which caused the feeling, blaming the world, blaming others, blaming themselves.

473
00:29:44,822 --> 00:29:47,304
[SPEAKER_01]: The point is, you're liable to feeling whether you want to be or not.

474
00:29:48,144 --> 00:29:52,367
[SPEAKER_01]: So you recognize, I can't, I don't have a say in that, and I should stop trying to change that.

475
00:29:53,608 --> 00:30:02,774
[SPEAKER_01]: And that reveals then, that what you can change, is then your own overly welcoming feeling, or overly resisting, based on whether they're agreeable or disagreeable.

476
00:30:03,634 --> 00:30:05,776
[SPEAKER_01]: Or being careless about them, if they're neutral.

477
00:30:06,376 --> 00:30:09,158
[SPEAKER_00]: Because suffering has access to you, if you do that.

478
00:30:10,393 --> 00:30:11,614
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that is what suffering is.

479
00:30:11,714 --> 00:30:13,115
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that access is the suffering.

480
00:30:13,535 --> 00:30:15,476
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's completely on you, not on the feeling.

481
00:30:16,076 --> 00:30:20,459
[SPEAKER_01]: Craving is not in the feeling, inasmuch upādāna is not in the aggregates.

482
00:30:20,979 --> 00:30:22,620
[SPEAKER_01]: You look at the aggregates, you're not gonna see upādāna.

483
00:30:22,700 --> 00:30:23,681
[SPEAKER_01]: You're only gonna see aggregates.

484
00:30:26,803 --> 00:30:28,384
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's that desire and lust.

485
00:30:29,264 --> 00:30:30,245
[SPEAKER_01]: Desire, I want this.

486
00:30:30,385 --> 00:30:32,687
[SPEAKER_01]: Desire, I don't want this.

487
00:30:32,727 --> 00:30:35,128
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why craving is there, in regard to it.

488
00:30:36,109 --> 00:30:38,390
[SPEAKER_01]: Like a parasite, as Yana Vilatere would describe it.

489
00:30:40,087 --> 00:30:49,493
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why this question, as we've done before, you can also ask yourself, again, not for the sake of answer, but for the sake of establishing an enduring perspective.

490
00:30:50,053 --> 00:30:57,818
[SPEAKER_01]: What am I feeling right now, fundamentally, overall, at the most peripheral corners of my experience?

491
00:30:58,859 --> 00:31:00,400
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I okay, or am I not okay?

492
00:31:02,722 --> 00:31:08,225
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's where the feeling is, or that's where it stretches from.

493
00:31:08,265 --> 00:31:08,986
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'm okay.

494
00:31:09,166 --> 00:31:09,426
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

495
00:31:09,872 --> 00:31:10,833
[SPEAKER_01]: So, let that endure.

496
00:31:10,973 --> 00:31:11,413
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm fine.

497
00:31:12,473 --> 00:31:16,416
[SPEAKER_01]: If you allow it to endure, then you see that phenomenon enduring for what it is.

498
00:31:16,836 --> 00:31:18,577
[SPEAKER_01]: And when it changes, you see that too.

499
00:31:19,217 --> 00:31:22,159
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like, now it has changed, and you're just like, oh, now I'm not okay.

500
00:31:23,439 --> 00:31:28,402
[SPEAKER_01]: So, now there is another sort of vessel of your mood that's holding you up, now different.

501
00:31:29,222 --> 00:31:32,204
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, so, before it was okay, now it's not okay.

502
00:31:32,984 --> 00:31:34,225
[SPEAKER_01]: Did I have a say in between?

503
00:31:34,305 --> 00:31:34,445
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

504
00:31:34,685 --> 00:31:36,046
[SPEAKER_01]: I didn't even see how it changed.

505
00:31:37,427 --> 00:31:38,007
[SPEAKER_01]: It just came.

506
00:31:40,084 --> 00:31:45,207
[SPEAKER_01]: Also, that means that whatever has come, it will have to go for the same reason that it came on its own.

507
00:31:45,708 --> 00:31:46,308
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's true.

508
00:31:46,348 --> 00:31:48,950
[SPEAKER_01]: That's already a form of, like, a liberating insight.

509
00:31:51,732 --> 00:32:00,618
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, you also realize that nothing is worth desiring towards, nothing is worth adhering to, because, yeah, because it came on its own, it will have to go on its own.

510
00:32:01,378 --> 00:32:07,022
[SPEAKER_01]: But it needs to be seen on that most general contextual level, universal level, not on the level of

511
00:32:07,587 --> 00:32:09,568
[SPEAKER_01]: An answer and the theory right in front of you.

512
00:32:09,728 --> 00:32:11,629
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, everything is constantly changing and so on.

513
00:32:12,749 --> 00:32:15,650
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if your present feeling now is I'm okay, that's fine.

514
00:32:16,511 --> 00:32:21,973
[SPEAKER_01]: Is this present okay feeling, as in agreeable feeling, is this liable to suffering?

515
00:32:22,113 --> 00:32:27,175
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, is it liable to change toward... into unpleasant feeling?

516
00:32:27,935 --> 00:32:29,316
[SPEAKER_01]: And I will suffer on account of it.

517
00:32:29,696 --> 00:32:30,316
[SPEAKER_01]: You will know that.

518
00:32:33,780 --> 00:32:39,384
[SPEAKER_01]: And that you realize that suffering is on that level of that liability, not necessarily on the presently enduring feeling.

519
00:32:41,685 --> 00:32:45,848
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you have suffering in the pleasure as well, by not having enough of the pleasure you crave.

520
00:32:47,849 --> 00:32:54,393
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you have suffering in the displeasure, because you don't want any of the displeasure, yet there is still some.

521
00:32:55,534 --> 00:33:00,837
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you stop exercising that attitude towards wanting more or not wanting any of it,

522
00:33:02,109 --> 00:33:06,632
[SPEAKER_01]: And seeing it for what it is, enduring on its own, you stop resisting it, you stop indulging it.

523
00:33:06,672 --> 00:33:11,155
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, you will stop providing the basis for craving towards the feeling.

524
00:33:12,055 --> 00:33:19,560
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you free yourself from suffering.

525
00:33:21,622 --> 00:33:23,603
[SPEAKER_01]: And now you ask yourself, how am I feeling right now?

526
00:33:24,764 --> 00:33:25,264
[SPEAKER_01]: Still okay.

527
00:33:25,404 --> 00:33:26,645
[SPEAKER_01]: Still the same feeling, enduring.

528
00:33:26,785 --> 00:33:27,465
[SPEAKER_01]: Ten minutes now.

529
00:33:29,186 --> 00:33:31,488
[SPEAKER_01]: And when, when it changes, you can carry on.

530
00:33:34,682 --> 00:33:37,623
[SPEAKER_01]: And when I'm not okay, will I be bothered by it?

531
00:33:40,324 --> 00:33:40,745
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, no.

532
00:33:40,985 --> 00:33:41,305
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

533
00:33:42,265 --> 00:33:42,805
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not sure.

534
00:33:43,646 --> 00:33:44,846
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, should I be sure?

535
00:33:46,207 --> 00:33:49,228
[SPEAKER_01]: Surely, I should strive for not being bothered by any feeling.

536
00:33:56,271 --> 00:34:02,574
[SPEAKER_01]: So, by questioning rightly, inducing perspective rightly, you get to induce more perspectives.

537
00:34:03,697 --> 00:34:05,218
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, as in...

538
00:34:05,238 --> 00:34:06,940
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, so this is what matters to me, then.

539
00:34:06,980 --> 00:34:09,061
[SPEAKER_01]: You get to clarify what needs to be done as well.

540
00:34:12,724 --> 00:34:18,228
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you also get to recognize important as important, essential as essential.

541
00:34:18,729 --> 00:34:20,070
[SPEAKER_01]: As you read that dhammapāda verse.

542
00:34:21,931 --> 00:34:32,640
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you realize, okay, so if I never lose the sight of presently enduring feeling that's independent of me, can I then have any careless attitude towards it?

543
00:34:32,960 --> 00:34:33,100
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

544
00:34:33,731 --> 00:34:36,132
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, so that's the gatekeeper that the Buddha was talking about.

545
00:34:37,732 --> 00:34:38,353
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't need to be...

546
00:34:38,793 --> 00:34:39,553
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm feeling okay.

547
00:34:39,653 --> 00:34:41,014
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm feeling... That's not a gatekeeper.

548
00:34:41,034 --> 00:34:43,594
[SPEAKER_01]: That's you fabricating the answer you think it is.

549
00:34:44,255 --> 00:34:46,736
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the feeling is already... You are already feeling okay.

550
00:34:47,136 --> 00:34:47,756
[SPEAKER_01]: Or not okay.

551
00:34:47,776 --> 00:34:48,076
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever.

552
00:34:48,096 --> 00:34:48,816
[SPEAKER_01]: You're already...

553
00:34:48,896 --> 00:34:53,778
[SPEAKER_01]: Feeling is already there independent of you, presently enduring in your experience.

554
00:34:55,243 --> 00:35:05,486
[SPEAKER_01]: If you stay mindful on that peripheral level, without making it into actual answer, but without losing sight of it, you can't exercise attitude towards, like, wanting it, not wanting it.

555
00:35:06,327 --> 00:35:07,607
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you realize this is completely...

556
00:35:08,648 --> 00:35:10,228
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm invisible to my own feelings.

557
00:35:10,288 --> 00:35:11,148
[SPEAKER_01]: They don't care about me.

558
00:35:11,208 --> 00:35:12,229
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't exist for them.

559
00:35:13,009 --> 00:35:15,890
[SPEAKER_01]: So, me trying to change them, they're just pointless.

560
00:35:15,930 --> 00:35:17,951
[SPEAKER_01]: Just me tying my own my own self in a knot.

561
00:35:18,351 --> 00:35:21,952
[SPEAKER_01]: And yet, feeling still remains unaffected for as long as he wants to remain.

562
00:35:22,437 --> 00:35:28,039
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why Arahant would remain contemplating five arising and falling, like enduring with the five aggregates on their own terms.

563
00:35:28,879 --> 00:35:29,720
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing for him to do.

564
00:35:32,201 --> 00:35:34,602
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you realize, oh, that's the task I should develop.

565
00:35:35,202 --> 00:35:38,723
[SPEAKER_01]: Not lose the sight of this general context of the present enduring feeling.

566
00:35:39,283 --> 00:35:44,145
[SPEAKER_01]: And see, it will become mechanical if you forget how you're actually feeling.

567
00:35:45,726 --> 00:35:49,948
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you keep... if you don't forget, or if you remind yourself, you're like, oh, I'm feeling like this.

568
00:35:49,988 --> 00:35:50,288
[SPEAKER_01]: Wait a minute.

569
00:35:50,828 --> 00:35:51,068
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I?

570
00:35:52,106 --> 00:35:53,186
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the presently enduring feeling?

571
00:35:53,207 --> 00:35:53,887
[SPEAKER_01]: What am I feeling?

572
00:35:56,208 --> 00:35:57,028
[SPEAKER_01]: Plus, minus.

573
00:35:57,328 --> 00:35:58,229
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, not okay.

574
00:35:58,449 --> 00:35:58,829
[SPEAKER_01]: Neutral.

575
00:35:58,929 --> 00:35:59,309
[SPEAKER_01]: What is it?

576
00:35:59,889 --> 00:36:01,290
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, discern that basis.

577
00:36:01,830 --> 00:36:03,851
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything else, the context is back, fully.

578
00:36:04,471 --> 00:36:08,073
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to go through the motions of the answers that popped before.

579
00:36:08,773 --> 00:36:10,474
[SPEAKER_01]: Or read the suttas to re-induce that.

580
00:36:10,514 --> 00:36:10,634
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

581
00:36:10,834 --> 00:36:11,355
[SPEAKER_01]: It's right there.

582
00:36:11,415 --> 00:36:12,295
[SPEAKER_01]: It was always right there.

583
00:36:15,176 --> 00:36:16,937
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you have a gatekeeper.

584
00:36:19,195 --> 00:36:24,019
[SPEAKER_01]: Should I be walking slowly and try not to lapse for a single second?

585
00:36:24,380 --> 00:36:24,520
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

586
00:36:24,900 --> 00:36:25,500
[SPEAKER_01]: That's pointless.

587
00:36:25,581 --> 00:36:26,581
[SPEAKER_01]: That's like misdirected.

588
00:36:27,042 --> 00:36:32,146
[SPEAKER_01]: All I need to do is not forget that presently enduring feeling is out of my control.

589
00:36:33,827 --> 00:36:41,513
[SPEAKER_01]: And I will refrain from carelessly indulging it, and I will refrain from carelessly trying to get rid of it.

590
00:36:42,955 --> 00:36:48,479
[SPEAKER_01]: And now I can talk to you, because that doesn't affect my attitude towards this feeling, doesn't induce the attitude towards this feeling.

591
00:36:49,042 --> 00:36:59,966
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you say, hey, let's go and, you know, do these things, or something unwholesome, or let's go and... in a big crowd of people, and it's like, well...

592
00:36:59,986 --> 00:37:08,889
[SPEAKER_01]: I might not necessarily do anything bad in that situation, but that situation is certainly very incompatible with me maintaining the perspective on the presently enduring feeling that's right here.

593
00:37:09,089 --> 00:37:10,089
[SPEAKER_01]: It's too distracting.

594
00:37:10,529 --> 00:37:11,430
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I say no to that.

595
00:37:12,310 --> 00:37:13,771
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, let's go to a Buddhist conference.

596
00:37:14,411 --> 00:37:16,031
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, you know, Buddhism, it's a good thing.

597
00:37:16,331 --> 00:37:18,072
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but that's three thousand people there.

598
00:37:18,498 --> 00:37:20,139
[SPEAKER_01]: Men, women, noise, smells, crowd.

599
00:37:20,599 --> 00:37:21,480
[SPEAKER_01]: What's the purpose of that?

600
00:37:22,841 --> 00:37:23,441
[SPEAKER_01]: What will I learn?

601
00:37:25,362 --> 00:37:27,383
[SPEAKER_01]: Will I learn anything I don't know?

602
00:37:27,443 --> 00:37:27,663
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

603
00:37:28,244 --> 00:37:32,966
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is... what is... ah, rooted in justified excitement, justified distraction.

604
00:37:33,427 --> 00:37:34,947
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, you know, it's a good cause.

605
00:37:35,888 --> 00:37:41,771
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but it's a good cause universalizing, like, conceptually for people.

606
00:37:41,951 --> 00:37:43,152
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a good cause on paper.

607
00:37:43,812 --> 00:37:46,474
[SPEAKER_01]: But for me, it's a cause for distraction.

608
00:37:47,655 --> 00:37:48,255
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I don't do it.

609
00:37:49,229 --> 00:37:59,237
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if I distract... and that's that's why you are responsible for your actions, that you're responsible for the basis of suffering in your own experience.

610
00:37:59,377 --> 00:38:03,841
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you can't accidentally give rise to greed, aversion, delusion.

611
00:38:04,642 --> 00:38:05,382
[SPEAKER_01]: It's always a choice.

612
00:38:07,164 --> 00:38:08,385
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you can also uproot it.

613
00:38:09,946 --> 00:38:11,267
[SPEAKER_01]: You choose to be careless.

614
00:38:11,808 --> 00:38:18,033
[SPEAKER_01]: You choose to do actions that will then result in losing the perspective and then do things out of lust or anger.

615
00:38:19,427 --> 00:38:20,728
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah.

616
00:38:20,948 --> 00:38:26,653
[SPEAKER_01]: You were fully aware of that leap that you made into the environment of lust, or anger, or distraction.

617
00:38:28,394 --> 00:38:30,055
[SPEAKER_01]: You just lied to yourself at the time.

618
00:38:30,075 --> 00:38:35,419
[SPEAKER_01]: But again, you can't truly lie to yourself, because in order to lie, you need to cover up the truth.

619
00:38:35,919 --> 00:38:40,663
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means you already know what the truth is, but you choose to focus on the lie.

620
00:38:41,564 --> 00:38:41,964
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no, no.

621
00:38:42,004 --> 00:38:42,865
[SPEAKER_01]: It's for a good cause.

622
00:38:43,765 --> 00:38:44,766
[SPEAKER_01]: It's for a greater cause.

623
00:38:45,240 --> 00:38:49,161
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no greater cause than personal suffering and freedom from it, for everyone.

624
00:38:51,821 --> 00:38:52,422
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

625
00:38:52,702 --> 00:38:55,122
[SPEAKER_01]: Through questioning, you get to discern a perspective.

626
00:38:55,362 --> 00:38:58,743
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you realize, oh, this is that... that task that I need to do.

627
00:39:00,043 --> 00:39:01,583
[SPEAKER_01]: I've been overdoing it, underdoing it.

628
00:39:01,603 --> 00:39:02,544
[SPEAKER_01]: I didn't know what I was doing.

629
00:39:02,564 --> 00:39:09,925
[SPEAKER_01]: But, if I protect this mind on the level of this peripheral, presently enduring feeling, independent of me, I don't need to do anything else.

630
00:39:10,525 --> 00:39:13,286
[SPEAKER_01]: And whatever I do, that doesn't contradict this, I'm fine.

631
00:39:14,376 --> 00:39:20,541
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you get in the sutra sometimes instruction on the level, whatever you know it's unwholesome, unskillful, incompatible, abandon it.

632
00:39:21,302 --> 00:39:26,766
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever you know that it's wholesome, skillful and compatible, cultivate it.

633
00:39:26,886 --> 00:39:29,208
[SPEAKER_01]: Cultivate it, as in let it remain in the being.

634
00:39:29,348 --> 00:39:30,109
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't negate it.

635
00:39:30,529 --> 00:39:31,450
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't go the opposite way.

636
00:39:38,443 --> 00:39:44,524
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly how you will end up discerning essential as essential, non-essential, which is samādhi, which is the right view.

637
00:39:45,624 --> 00:39:48,705
[SPEAKER_01]: Sotāpanna knows skillful as skillful, unskillful as unskillful.

638
00:39:48,725 --> 00:39:51,185
[SPEAKER_01]: He knows essential as essential, non-essential as non-essential.

639
00:39:54,146 --> 00:39:59,467
[SPEAKER_01]: Because he sees direct connection to that basis that used to provide the suffering.

640
00:39:59,667 --> 00:40:03,767
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, this was fueling the basis, this is removing the basis.

641
00:40:03,827 --> 00:40:07,088
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, practicing for dispassion, removal of desire and lust.

642
00:40:08,305 --> 00:40:10,045
[SPEAKER_01]: Removal of any basis of unwholesome.

643
00:40:15,426 --> 00:40:21,287
[SPEAKER_01]: But again, one thing is to have these on paper as the information which is necessary, something to begin with.

644
00:40:21,947 --> 00:40:30,249
[SPEAKER_01]: But another thing is to recognize that you must be the one understanding it on the basis of your present experience, not on a basis on emotionally pleasing answer.

645
00:40:32,269 --> 00:40:35,830
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can look at your whole day, in a way, day ahead of you.

646
00:40:37,378 --> 00:40:43,161
[SPEAKER_01]: You can look from that... from having established that more general perspective.

647
00:40:44,502 --> 00:41:00,691
[SPEAKER_01]: All the particular actions that you're going to do throughout the day, you can... you can keep this perspective at the back of your mind, which means you will not be able to be too un... too... too careless about your acts, because the perspective you maintain

648
00:41:01,400 --> 00:41:07,264
[SPEAKER_01]: Still maintains a degree of context towards your particular actions, even if you don't actively think about it all the time.

649
00:41:08,585 --> 00:41:17,391
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if my context is freedom from suffering, I want to be free from suffering, means my context is also, by the extension, renunciation.

650
00:41:18,671 --> 00:41:21,473
[SPEAKER_01]: So, my context is saying no to gratuitous desire.

651
00:41:23,314 --> 00:41:28,418
[SPEAKER_01]: And again, you don't need to go, like now, I'm going to my kuti and I'm walking and I must say no to desire.

652
00:41:28,458 --> 00:41:28,578
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

653
00:41:28,738 --> 00:41:30,299
[SPEAKER_01]: My context is renunciation.

654
00:41:32,151 --> 00:41:37,352
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't get distracted by overly defining it, but certainly don't forget about it or mystify it.

655
00:41:38,793 --> 00:41:48,296
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if your mind is established upon the theme of renunciation, anything that's non-renunciation, on the particular level, will not take your mind away from it.

656
00:41:49,696 --> 00:41:56,898
[SPEAKER_01]: That's very well meant by establishing the mind upon that greater context, like you establish your mind upon the perceptions of the forest,

657
00:41:57,493 --> 00:42:04,715
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when you're in the village, no sights and sounds from the village will take your mind away, because you established it, you made the effort to establish the context.

658
00:42:04,995 --> 00:42:07,535
[SPEAKER_01]: So, because what is the... what are the perceptions of the forest?

659
00:42:08,856 --> 00:42:16,758
[SPEAKER_01]: It's perception of solitude, perception of... like absence of people, perception of non-sensuality.

660
00:42:16,778 --> 00:42:18,898
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no sensuality in the forest, no sense objects.

661
00:42:19,878 --> 00:42:21,079
[SPEAKER_01]: Perception of hardship.

662
00:42:22,119 --> 00:42:27,080
[SPEAKER_01]: And those are the things that you cultivate and you start to value and you want to maintain at the back of your mind.

663
00:42:29,613 --> 00:42:33,155
[SPEAKER_01]: You maintain that in the back of your mind, you don't need to worry about what comes in front.

664
00:42:33,355 --> 00:42:38,198
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you... you... this is... that's the gatekeeper, that's the... that's the entry point.

665
00:42:38,399 --> 00:42:49,005
[SPEAKER_01]: And if that's kept sort of under a watchful eye rightly, not like making it into sort of a meditation object, just keep it as a context, that's it.

666
00:42:49,045 --> 00:42:53,348
[SPEAKER_01]: You can go throughout your day without having to worry about particular things.

667
00:42:55,309 --> 00:42:57,251
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you lapse, you can re-establish the context.

668
00:42:57,551 --> 00:42:58,371
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's always there.

669
00:42:58,792 --> 00:42:59,272
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the point.

670
00:43:02,081 --> 00:43:06,724
[SPEAKER_01]: The universals of renunciation, freedom from suffering, it's on the basis of any present experience.

671
00:43:06,764 --> 00:43:08,785
[SPEAKER_01]: And there's always present experience for as long as you're alive.

672
00:43:09,485 --> 00:43:11,867
[SPEAKER_01]: These things I'm saying now, it doesn't make sense to somebody.

673
00:43:13,568 --> 00:43:14,868
[SPEAKER_01]: You can make that into a thing.

674
00:43:16,069 --> 00:43:19,571
[SPEAKER_01]: Also, so I have this pressure to understand what I just listened to.

675
00:43:20,932 --> 00:43:21,152
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

676
00:43:22,913 --> 00:43:24,434
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, because I want to be free from suffering.

677
00:43:24,614 --> 00:43:27,696
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, so what I just listened to was talking about freedom from suffering.

678
00:43:28,436 --> 00:43:29,557
[SPEAKER_01]: And I want to be free from suffering.

679
00:43:30,077 --> 00:43:31,398
[SPEAKER_01]: So why don't I try and understand it,

680
00:43:31,902 --> 00:43:37,526
[SPEAKER_01]: Where my suffering is right now, not where my mind takes me in terms of, oh, this is what is meant and this is what is meant.

681
00:43:37,566 --> 00:43:37,726
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

682
00:43:38,086 --> 00:43:42,029
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I want to understand this, because I want to be free from suffering, right here and now.

683
00:43:42,930 --> 00:43:45,512
[SPEAKER_01]: So, where is this suffering right here and now that needs to be understood?

684
00:43:46,733 --> 00:43:48,454
[SPEAKER_01]: And then we go back to what we spoke before.

685
00:43:48,894 --> 00:43:50,035
[SPEAKER_01]: How am I feeling right now then?

686
00:43:51,676 --> 00:44:00,803
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're questioning, you're contemplation, you don't want to do it in that, like a, like a, like a third-person sense, when you're just thinking about it, on designation, on top of designation, on top

687
00:44:01,582 --> 00:44:05,403
[SPEAKER_01]: Failing to see what each of those designations actually designates in the present moment.

688
00:44:06,403 --> 00:44:09,664
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you develop concrete thinking, not abstract thinking.

689
00:44:09,704 --> 00:44:15,765
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why when you speak like this, for people who have no concept of thinking concretely, they will just think this is just all theory.

690
00:44:17,486 --> 00:44:20,226
[SPEAKER_01]: And you make things concrete by having a jhāna experience.

691
00:44:20,606 --> 00:44:22,267
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, what they think jhāna is.

692
00:44:22,927 --> 00:44:24,867
[SPEAKER_01]: But jhāna is the right thinking.

693
00:44:24,887 --> 00:44:31,509
[SPEAKER_01]: Thinking and pondering that's impenetrable to desire and lust, that has mounted the entire central domain, as the Buddha described it.

694
00:44:32,697 --> 00:44:34,678
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the jhāna, unshakable thinking.

695
00:44:34,958 --> 00:44:36,778
[SPEAKER_01]: Concrete thinking results in jhāna.

696
00:44:36,798 --> 00:44:37,499
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what jhāna is.

697
00:44:38,979 --> 00:44:39,439
[SPEAKER_01]: Not abstract.

698
00:44:39,459 --> 00:44:41,580
[SPEAKER_01]: But most people, thinking is abstract for them.

699
00:44:42,080 --> 00:44:47,262
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, it's divorced from the present concrete situation.

700
00:44:47,282 --> 00:44:53,524
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you wanna re-discern it on the right terms and see and think concretely.

701
00:44:53,544 --> 00:44:54,064
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I mean.

702
00:44:54,304 --> 00:45:01,407
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't jump into the answer that's gonna be on the level of abstract, but discern it on the level of presently arising situation that's concrete, that's fact.

703
00:45:02,993 --> 00:45:03,553
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I am...

704
00:45:03,573 --> 00:45:03,874
[SPEAKER_01]: I am...

705
00:45:03,914 --> 00:45:04,794
[SPEAKER_01]: I am okay overall.

706
00:45:05,995 --> 00:45:07,295
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you are clearly thinking it.

707
00:45:07,956 --> 00:45:11,338
[SPEAKER_01]: All these are designations, but they pertain to the presently enduring phenomena.

708
00:45:12,418 --> 00:45:14,880
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you go, oh, yes, I am okay, but...

709
00:45:14,920 --> 00:45:16,240
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, wait a minute, am I okay?

710
00:45:17,561 --> 00:45:23,905
[SPEAKER_01]: Means you thinking you're okay and building everything else on top of it, took you away from presently, because you started doubting it.

711
00:45:26,106 --> 00:45:27,987
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you can't doubt the concrete phenomena.

712
00:45:29,092 --> 00:45:29,993
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, is this like this?

713
00:45:30,013 --> 00:45:30,453
[SPEAKER_01]: Is this like this?

714
00:45:30,933 --> 00:45:32,334
[SPEAKER_01]: What's my present experience as a whole?

715
00:45:32,414 --> 00:45:33,675
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, it's the present experience of doubt.

716
00:45:34,515 --> 00:45:35,716
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's beyond doubt.

717
00:45:38,057 --> 00:45:44,141
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when concrete thinking becomes your norm, you pretty much can surmount any hindrance that comes your way.

718
00:45:47,062 --> 00:45:54,126
[SPEAKER_01]: Because whatever hindrance is, it's a hindrance in regard to, like, that which pressures you with, or takes you towards, or confuses you about.

719
00:45:54,707 --> 00:45:56,968
[SPEAKER_01]: But it cannot be a hindrance in regard to its own presence.

720
00:45:58,020 --> 00:46:04,666
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the mindfulness practice, rightly, he knows his mind is mind affected by lust, or mind free from lust.

721
00:46:06,228 --> 00:46:12,714
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, lust is present, but it's not, like, by thinking it concretely, you're not affected by that lust.

722
00:46:13,454 --> 00:46:22,643
[SPEAKER_01]: Losing the sight of that concrete... That's why there is a degree of, like, a temporary liberation, even though people are utterly confused in their views, and just keep saying, just be present, be here now.

723
00:46:24,314 --> 00:46:26,035
[SPEAKER_01]: It brings you back into that concreteness.

724
00:46:26,095 --> 00:46:27,736
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why it does have a release.

725
00:46:28,216 --> 00:46:29,076
[SPEAKER_01]: Nobody denies that.

726
00:46:29,476 --> 00:46:30,677
[SPEAKER_01]: But you want to understand it.

727
00:46:31,597 --> 00:46:34,939
[SPEAKER_01]: Not just, like, go back now, go back now, be present now.

728
00:46:34,979 --> 00:46:41,042
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there is no understanding, which means none of the habits that take you away from that concrete moment are being understood.

729
00:46:41,542 --> 00:46:44,443
[SPEAKER_01]: Having not being understood, you're not free from those.

730
00:46:44,523 --> 00:46:47,544
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means you're just, sort of, resetting the problem.

731
00:46:49,785 --> 00:46:53,007
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, come back to present moment, only to realize

732
00:46:53,518 --> 00:46:56,259
[SPEAKER_01]: That you can't be outside of a present moment, even if you want to.

733
00:46:56,960 --> 00:47:03,963
[SPEAKER_01]: Only to realize that the utmost of your abstract thinking is concrete in itself, as a present layer is an experience.

734
00:47:06,024 --> 00:47:14,269
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, all the things that you thought were abstract before, or pure magic, you realize, well, they were only magic, because I was the one divorcing them from reality.

735
00:47:14,329 --> 00:47:16,190
[SPEAKER_01]: But they, in themselves, they are real as such.

736
00:47:18,571 --> 00:47:22,953
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you can have my main bodies, and fly and glide the suns, and whatever else you want to do.

737
00:47:24,076 --> 00:47:25,257
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's not abstract anymore.

738
00:47:28,138 --> 00:47:31,901
[SPEAKER_01]: So, abstraction, it's more an attitude, rather than the content of your thought.

739
00:47:33,702 --> 00:47:39,665
[SPEAKER_01]: Let's say somebody who doesn't understand what you're talking about, but he hears your words, your designations, but he doesn't see what they designate in his experience.

740
00:47:40,146 --> 00:47:43,047
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's... that's abstraction for them.

741
00:47:43,628 --> 00:47:47,430
[SPEAKER_01]: But not because of what you said, but because of... they don't have the basis to discern this.

742
00:47:47,570 --> 00:47:49,391
[SPEAKER_01]: They haven't discerned the basis to... for these things.

743
00:47:51,292 --> 00:47:52,513
[SPEAKER_01]: So, let's say, how am I feeling right now?

744
00:47:53,314 --> 00:47:53,794
[SPEAKER_01]: By giving

745
00:47:54,399 --> 00:47:59,260
[SPEAKER_01]: A habitual answer takes you usually in the level of that abstraction.

746
00:48:00,160 --> 00:48:05,241
[SPEAKER_01]: But by looking how you're feeling right now and then answering it, well, that's concrete thinking now.

747
00:48:05,741 --> 00:48:09,342
[SPEAKER_01]: The thought has arisen on account of a concrete basis of a presently enduring feeling.

748
00:48:09,902 --> 00:48:10,962
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's where you want to stay.

749
00:48:11,102 --> 00:48:12,603
[SPEAKER_01]: That's that context I talk about.

750
00:48:15,823 --> 00:48:23,565
[SPEAKER_01]: And the context will cease to be the context when it has drifted into abstraction and lost any anchoring to the basis of concrete situation.

751
00:48:24,289 --> 00:48:28,058
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence mindfulness, anchoring, referencing, whatever.

752
00:48:28,660 --> 00:48:29,321
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the whole point.

753
00:48:32,289 --> 00:48:34,013
[SPEAKER_01]: The principle of it is what mindfulness is.

